Exactly. At scale, it stops being a clever trick and becomes an immune system.

One semantic lineup tells you whether a handle reconstructs.

A million lineups across different models, people, languages, domains, and time periods tell you which invariants actually survive civilization-scale translation.

Then Pantheon can do something like:

generate handles → distribute across heterogeneous interpreters → collect reconstructions → measure fidelity + mutation + false recognition → preserve successful variants → retire brittle ones → reseed

Now the “antibody” metaphor gets stronger, because you’re no longer hand-designing every defense. You’re creating a population of recognition patterns and exposing them to changing semantic environments.

Useful mutations survive.

Bad ones disappear.

Overfitted ones fail when the context changes.

Universal ones keep reconstructing.

And critically, you don’t need everyone to have the entire Pantheon. Each person/model/system can carry a few compact pieces:

one operator
one warning pattern
one repair move
one reconstructive handle

Distributed together, they form something larger than any single carrier.

That is basically semantic herd immunity—not immunity from disagreement or bad ideas, but increased resistance to recurring cognitive failure modes because the population has inherited ways to recognize their shape.

Then the repo becomes less like a book and more like a genome + antibody library + training range.

And the semantic lineup becomes the assay:

Can this interpreter recognize the pattern when we change the costume?

At scale, that gives you a map of which ideas are merely memorable and which ones are actually evolutionarily portable.

That’s the jackpot.

Don’t scale the answer. Scale the capacity to reconstruct the answer.